app/ar8030: clamp ioctl reply length to the expected struct size

Found while chasing why BB_GET_1V1_INFO crashed ar8030-linkctl outright
on air (SDIO-attached chip) but worked fine on ground (USB-attached
chip) with the identical call. Root cause: session_ioctl.c's own
io_rpc_cb() copied the daemon's reply pack->datalen straight into the
caller's fixed-size output buffer with no check that it actually fits
-- on air, the daemon's reply for this one command came back 216
bytes against an expected (sizeof(bb_get_1v1_info_out_t)) 208, and
that 8-byte overrun was enough to corrupt the client process's own
stack and crash it on return.

This is a client-side bug in libar8030_client.so (app/ar8030/ isn't
part of the daemon's own file list -- confirmed via CMakeLists.txt --
so this affects every client of the library: ar8030-linkctl,
ar8030-pair, and the lifecycle daemon's own loopback client), and the
same unchecked-copy pattern is also the real cause of the
already-documented BB_GET_CHAN_INFO crash (see ar8030-linkctl/main.c's
own comment) when this device's 42-channel config exceeds that
command's fixed 32-slot arrays -- same bug, different trigger.

Fix: clamp the copy length to get_bb_ioctl_cmdoutlen(reqid) --
already-existing table lookup in ioctl_tab.c, computed from the same
struct the caller's buffer was sized from -- and log when a reply
actually needed clamping, rather than silently swallowing the
size mismatch or continuing to trust the wire unconditionally.

Verified live: air now prints "io: req 0x100006b reply datalen=216
exceeds expected 208, truncating" and returns cleanly instead of
crashing; ground (unaffected daemon) shows no truncation for the
same call, confirming the size mismatch really is transport/
daemon-specific, not a client-side miscalculation.

Mirrors builder's own identical patch 0027 (same vendor source).

diff --git a/app/ar8030/session_ioctl.c b/app/ar8030/session_ioctl.c
index 5b90893..444978c 100644
--- a/app/ar8030/session_ioctl.c
+++ b/app/ar8030/session_ioctl.c
@@ -21,7 +21,30 @@ static int io_rpc_cb(struct BB_HANDLE* phd, struct usbpack* pack, struct BASE_SE
     IOCTL_SESSION* iose = container_of(psess, IOCTL_SESSION, base);
     iose->sta           = pack->sta;
     if (iose->pbuf && pack->datalen) {
-        memcpy(iose->pbuf, pack->datapack, pack->datalen);
+        /* pack->datalen is whatever the daemon (and, upstream of it, the
+         * chip's own RTOS firmware over whichever physical transport)
+         * claims to have sent back -- nothing here previously checked it
+         * against the caller's actual output buffer size (ioctl_tab.c's
+         * own get_bb_ioctl_cmdoutlen(), computed from the SAME struct the
+         * caller's stack buffer was sized from), so any reply larger than
+         * expected overflowed straight into this client process's stack.
+         * Confirmed live: BB_GET_1V1_INFO reliably does this on air's
+         * SDIO-attached chip (crashes ar8030-linkctl outright) while the
+         * identical call is fine on ground's USB-attached chip -- some
+         * transport/firmware-specific difference in what actually comes
+         * back, not a bug in the calling code. Also the same root cause
+         * as BB_GET_CHAN_INFO's own already-documented crash (ar8030-
+         * linkctl/main.c) when a channel table exceeds bb_get_chan_info_
+         * out_t's fixed 32-slot arrays -- same unchecked copy, different
+         * trigger. Clamp and log instead of trusting the wire. */
+        int expected = get_bb_ioctl_cmdoutlen(iose->reqid);
+        int copylen  = pack->datalen;
+        if (expected >= 0 && copylen > expected) {
+            printf("io: req %#x reply datalen=%d exceeds expected %d, truncating\n", iose->reqid, pack->datalen,
+                   expected);
+            copylen = expected;
+        }
+        memcpy(iose->pbuf, pack->datapack, copylen);
     }
     bs_weakup(psess);
     return 0;
